---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import World from "../../components/World.astro";
const palette = [
  { name: "Florets Yellow", hex: "#ffd11b" },
  { name: "Sky Blue", hex: "#8dcaff" },
  { name: "Leaf Green", hex: "#0d5500" },
  { name: "Daisy White", hex: "#fffaeb" },
  { name: "Deep Green", hex: "#001400" },
];
---

<Layout title="Behind the pixels — 16-Bit Con explorations">
  <header class="wrap drafts-header">
    <a href="/">
      <img src="/images/vega-logo.svg" alt="Vega" width="100" height="33" />
    </a>
    <a href="/drafts">↖ All drafts</a>
  </header>
  <main id="main" class="wrap">
    <section class="drafts-intro">
      <span class="mono">DESIGN WORKBOOK / SEPTEMBER 2026</span>
      <h1>
        Behind
        <br />
        the pixels.
      </h1>
      <p>
        Three directions, one small world. An open sketchbook of the
        composition, color, and interaction decisions behind 16-Bit Con.
      </p>
    </section>
    <div class="draft-directions">
      <article class="draft-card">
        <div class="draft-art draft-lavender">
          <span class="mono">01 / EARLY COLOR STUDY</span>
          <h2>
            16-Bit
            <br />
            Con ✳
          </h2>
          <p style="margin-top:35px">The future looks familiar.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="notes">
          <h3>Future nostalgia</h3>
          <p>
            An initial lavender poster study, imagined before receiving the
            brand file. The soft palette felt playful, but weakened Vega’s
            distinctive green and yellow. Kept the oversized typography; retired
            the color direction.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="draft-card">
        <div class="draft-art draft-terminal">
          <span class="mono">02 / TERMINAL STUDY</span>
          <h2>
            &gt; HELLO_
            <br />
            16-BIT
            <br />
            CON.
          </h2>
          <p class="mono" style="margin-top:35px">
            SYSTEM STATUS: READY TO PLAY
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="notes">
          <h3>Back to the terminal</h3>
          <p>
            Deep green, yellow prompts, and an operating-system metaphor. Strong
            nostalgia, but too much code imagery made the invitation feel
            exclusive to developers. Kept a little of this for the pixel studio
            window.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="draft-card">
        <div class="draft-art draft-world">
          <span class="mono">03 / SELECTED DIRECTION</span>
          <h2>
            A small
            <br />
            world.
          </h2>
          <World />
        </div>
        <div class="notes">
          <h3>A world worth exploring</h3>
          <p>
            The final direction combines the brand palette and Mondwest with an
            original miniature convention. An oversized computer becomes a
            meeting place. Technology feels human, social, and open to everyone.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
    <section class="draft-section" style="margin-top:65px">
      <span class="section-marker mono">THE FOUNDATION</span>
      <h2>Vega, down to the pixel.</h2>
      <p>
        Colors and logo come from the supplied Figma brand guidelines. PP
        Mondwest provides the pixel character; Geist keeps practical information
        clear. Geist Mono is reserved for timings, labels, and the drawing tool.
        The local font files match those served by Vega’s public website.
      </p>
      <div class="draft-token-grid">
        {palette.map((p) => (
          <div
            class="token"
            style={`background:${p.hex};color:${["#0d5500", "#001400"].includes(p.hex) ? "#fffaeb" : "#001400"}`}
          >
            <span>{p.name}</span>
            <span class="mono">{p.hex.toUpperCase()}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="draft-section">
      <h2>Composition before decoration.</h2>
      <p>
        A split poster lets the event name and registration stay immediately
        legible while the illustration gets room to breathe. On mobile, the
        reading order stays intact: event, date, place, then the world. A
        centered alternative competed with the illustration and pushed the CTA
        too far down.
      </p>
      <div class="wireframes">
        <pre>SELECTED / SPLIT POSTER
┌─────────────────────────────┐
│ VEGA     NAVIGATION    PASS │
├────────────┬────────────────┤
│ 16-BIT     │                │
│ CON        │  SMALL WORLD   │
│            │  ILLUSTRATION  │
│ GET PASS   │                │
│ DATE/PLACE │                │
├────────────┴────────────────┤
│ EXPERIENCE → LINEUP → PLAY  │
└─────────────────────────────┘</pre>
        <pre>EXPLORATION / CENTERED POSTER
┌─────────────────────────────┐
│ VEGA                   PASS │
│          16-BIT CON         │
│         SMALL PIXELS       │
│           BIG IDEAS        │
│      WORLD ILLUSTRATION    │
│          GET PASS          │
│         DATE / PLACE       │
├─────────────────────────────┤
│ Rejected: delayed action   │
└─────────────────────────────┘</pre>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="draft-section">
      <h2>Make the invitation playable.</h2>
      <p>
        The 16 × 16 pixel studio is the creative fourth section. It starts with
        a flower, connecting back to Vega, and gives visitors a tiny creative
        artifact to take away. Drawing works with a mouse, touch, or a keyboard.
        A souvenir pass brings the event concept to life without pretending to
        process real registrations.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="draft-section">
      <h2>The practical details.</h2>
      <p>
        Astro generates static HTML with a small TypeScript module for the
        canvas and pass dialog. No UI framework. No external runtime fonts. No
        tracking. Reduced motion is respected, native disclosure elements expose
        the agenda, and every drawing color has a text label.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:22px">
        Event date, location, and sessions are fictional editorial choices.
        Exact source assets and the original illustration component are included
        in the project.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:25px">
        <a
          class="text-button"
          href="https://www.figma.com/design/IOaTIe2PBjpjxujIvQX5aT/"
        >
          Open the duplicated Figma workbook ↗
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <footer class="wrap">
    <a href="/drafts">Return to all drafts ↗</a>
    <span class="mono">16-BIT CON / DESIGN EXPLORATIONS</span>
  </footer>
</Layout>
